/*
 * **Decorative modifications** — the festive transformations the game records in the
 * same field as an engineering blueprint, keyed by the Frontier `fdname` a journal
 * writes (`EngineerModifications` on a `StoredModules` entry, `Engineering.BlueprintName`
 * on a `Loadout` module).
 *
 * These are not engineering, which is why they are here rather than in `blueprints.jsonc`:
 * they have no grade, cost no materials and no engineer offers one — a module carrying one
 * was awarded already transformed. They are absent from `engineering-options.jsonc` for the
 * same reason, that file listing what a player may apply at an engineer. The ids are real
 * all the same, and a build carrying one has to resolve to something rather than to
 * nothing; that is what this file is for.
 *
 * **They are not cosmetic-only.** A festive launcher fires fireworks rather than flak, and
 * the transformation cuts the module's `Damage` by 99% to match — the one stat any of them
 * moves. That is stored, so a record here is never to be read as "this changes no stat".
 *
 * Sources: the ids, and the module they sit on, are read off a `StoredModules` capture
 *          contributed by the repository owner (521 stored modules, 2026-08-07 UTC,
 *          scrubbed of the commander, carrier and market it came from), which holds three
 *          medium turreted Remote Release Flak Launchers, one per colour. That they were
 *          awarded already transformed rather than engineered, and that the game calls
 *          them festive launchers, are the contributor's account — a `StoredModules` entry
 *          carries neither. The `Damage` modifier is read off the same contributor's
 *          outfitting panel, which shows the transformation at -99.0% and the resulting
 *          launcher at 0.3 damage, 0.2/s. Those three figures settle the method between
 *          them: -99.0% of the medium turreted launcher's 34 base damage is 0.34, which
 *          the panel rounds to 0.3, and 0.34 x its 0.5 rate of fire is 0.17, rounded to
 *          0.2/s. An overwrite to a flat 0.3 would be -99.1% and 0.1/s, so the modifier is
 *          multiplicative and the panel's 0.3 is a rounding of 0.34. EDSY
 *          (github.com/taleden/EDSY, taleden, CC BY-NC 4.0) lists the same three
 *          transformations and gives them no modifiers, which the cut shows to be an
 *          incomplete record rather than a second opinion. The transformations
 *          themselves are Elite Dangerous data, property of Frontier Developments plc,
 *          under Frontier's media-usage terms:
 *          https://forums.frontier.co.uk/threads/510879/
 *
 * Each record is { name, modules, modifiers }. `name` pairs the festive naming with the
 * colour the id spells; no registry publishes the outfitting panel's own string. `modules`
 * lists every module symbol observed carrying the transformation, joined to the module
 * catalogues; it is what has been seen, not a claim about what the game could put one on.
 * A modifier is { label, method, value }: the journal Modifier Label, how it applies, and
 * a fixed value — the same vocabulary `pre-engineered.jsonc` uses, and for the same
 * reason, a transformation that arrives on the module being a fixed article rather than a
 * roll.
 *
 * Full provenance: data/ships/SOURCES.md, §Decorative modifications.
 */
{
  "Decorative_Green": {
    "name": "Festive Green",
    "modules": ["Hpt_FlakMortar_Turret_Medium"],
    "modifiers": [{ "label": "Damage", "method": "multiplicative", "value": -0.99 }]
  },
  "Decorative_Red": {
    "name": "Festive Red",
    "modules": ["Hpt_FlakMortar_Turret_Medium"],
    "modifiers": [{ "label": "Damage", "method": "multiplicative", "value": -0.99 }]
  },
  "Decorative_Yellow": {
    "name": "Festive Yellow",
    "modules": ["Hpt_FlakMortar_Turret_Medium"],
    "modifiers": [{ "label": "Damage", "method": "multiplicative", "value": -0.99 }]
  }
}
